# Runtime-only image assembly from binaries built outside Docker (ADR-0034,
# decision 7).
#
# This is the CI companion to the root Dockerfile, not a replacement for it.
# The root Dockerfile is self-contained: its builder stage compiles the
# workspace from scratch inside Docker, which is what a release build and a
# from-nothing local build need. That is also its cost -- a cold release build
# of this workspace with the builder's CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=2 cap took 57 minutes
# on the 8 GiB arm64 development host, and it cannot reuse the host's cargo,
# sccache, or GitHub Actions cache at all.
#
# The `k8s-integration` job in .github/workflows/ci.yml therefore builds the
# release binaries on the runner, where the shared sccache and
# Swatinem/rust-cache caches already hold most of the workspace, and assembles
# the images from those binaries here. `scripts/kind-up.sh
# RAVEL_SKIP_IMAGE_BUILD=1` exists for exactly this split.
#
# The build context is a directory holding just the two binaries (the repo root
# cannot be used: .dockerignore excludes target/ from it, deliberately, so that
# build artifacts never enter the root build context). Both stages otherwise
# mirror the root Dockerfile's runtime stages -- same paths, same entrypoints --
# so the lane tests the image that ships as closely as it can. Two deliberate
# differences, both recorded here rather than left to be rediscovered:
#
# 1. No ravel-cli in the server stage. Nothing in the operator, the manifests,
#    or scripts/kind-demo.sh invokes it, so building a third release binary
#    would lengthen the job without covering anything. The shipping server
#    image (root Dockerfile, `--target server`) still includes it.
#
# 2. The runtime base is Debian 13 (glibc 2.41), not the shipping image's
#    Debian 12 (glibc 2.36). Forced, not preferred: these binaries are built on
#    the build host, and the ubuntu-latest runner is Ubuntu 24.04 with glibc
#    2.39, so its binaries reference symbol versions Debian 12 does not have.
#    Measured on an Ubuntu 24.04 (glibc 2.39) host, both binaries failed to
#    exec on gcr.io/distroless/cc-debian12:nonroot:
#      ravel-server:   version `GLIBC_2.38' not found
#                      (__isoc23_sscanf, __isoc23_strtol -- from C dependencies
#                      compiled against the build host's headers)
#      ravel-operator: version `GLIBC_2.39' not found
#                      (pidfd_getpid, pidfd_spawnp -- from Rust std)
#    The shipping image is unaffected: its builder stage compiles inside
#    rust:1.97.1-bookworm, so it produces and runs on the same glibc 2.36. The
#    alternatives were pinning this one job to an older runner image (a hidden
#    coupling that breaks when that image is retired) or rebuilding inside a
#    bookworm container in CI (which throws away the warm cache this split
#    exists to use). Override RUNTIME_BASE to build against the shipping base
#    when the build host's glibc is old enough:
#      docker build --build-arg RUNTIME_BASE=gcr.io/distroless/cc-debian12:nonroot ...
#
# Usage (see the `k8s-integration` job for the real invocation):
#
#   ctx=$(mktemp -d)
#   cp target/release/ravel-server target/release/ravel-operator "$ctx/"
#   docker build --file Dockerfile.prebuilt --target server \
#     --tag ravel-server:kind-ci "$ctx"
#   docker build --file Dockerfile.prebuilt --target operator \
#     --tag ravel-operator:kind-ci "$ctx"
#
# The binaries must be Linux binaries for the target architecture, dynamically
# linked against a glibc no newer than the base image's. The job smoke-runs
# `--help` in both images immediately after assembly, so a mismatch surfaces as
# the dynamic linker's own message rather than as a pod CrashLoopBackOff minutes
# later. That check is what turned the glibc problem above from a k8s-lane
# mystery into a one-line error.
ARG RUNTIME_BASE=gcr.io/distroless/cc-debian13:nonroot

# ---- Runtime: server image --------------------------------------------------
FROM ${RUNTIME_BASE} AS server

COPY ravel-server /usr/local/bin/ravel-server

# Documentation only: HTTP (OTLP/HTTP, health) and gRPC (OTLP/gRPC) ports.
EXPOSE 4318 4317

# No default CMD, matching the root Dockerfile: the operator supplies every
# argument from the CRD.
ENTRYPOINT ["/usr/local/bin/ravel-server"]

# ---- Runtime: operator image ------------------------------------------------
FROM ${RUNTIME_BASE} AS operator

COPY ravel-operator /usr/local/bin/ravel-operator

ENTRYPOINT ["/usr/local/bin/ravel-operator"]
